  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Both exist in Kibana. I couldn't run a direct query to confirm the 5xx filter matches real documents: my one attempt through Kibana's console proxy was rejected as unauthorised (401).\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) has one document per web request. The main fields are:\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`.\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string with a `.keyword` version) and `bytes`.\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`.\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `hour_of_day`.\n\n**2. Dashboard: `EFF-a5e706fb Web logs overview`** (id `eff-a5e706fb-web-logs-overview-52a113b51081`). It has 8 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Requests by response code over time (stacked bar chart)\n- Top 10 URLs with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Requests by source country, and requests by operating system (pie charts)\n\n**3. Alert rule: `EFF-a5e706fb 5xx spike`** (id `d67a775b-cfdc-4076-a9b9-38e6494afde4`).\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts documents whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" (any 5xx code) over the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n- It's enabled. After its first run its status is `ok`, which means the query ran without errors, but not that it matched any 5xx requests.\n- I tagged it `EFF-a5e706fb`. It has no notification actions attached, so when it fires it creates an alert in Kibana but doesn't send a message anywhere.",
